+# Forking Base in this checkout
+
+We fork Base because our contract reads USDC at
+`0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913` — a Base address. The onboarding
+note's command does not fork Base, and its sanity check cannot tell the
+difference.
+
+## What the onboarding note gets wrong
+
+`yarn fork base` silently forks **Ethereum mainnet**, not Base.
+
+The chain of scripts, quoted from this repo:
+
+`package.json` (repo root):
+
+```json
+"fork": "yarn foundry:fork",
+"foundry:fork": "yarn workspace @se-2/foundry fork",
+```
+
+`packages/foundry/package.json`:
+
+```json
+"fork": "make fork FORK_URL=${1:-mainnet}",
+```
+
+`packages/foundry/Makefile:14-16`:
+
+```make
+# Start a fork
+fork: setup-anvil-wallet
+	anvil --fork-url ${FORK_URL} --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+The script body is run by a shell, and yarn binds the **first** token after the
+script name to `$0`, not `$1`. So in `yarn fork base`, `base` lands in `$0`,
+`$1` is unset, and `${1:-mainnet}` falls through to its default:
+
+```
+$ sh -c 'echo "FORK_URL=${1:-mainnet} (\$0=$0)"' base
+FORK_URL=mainnet ($0=base)
+```
+
+Running it here prints the exact anvil invocation make executes:
+
+```
+$ yarn fork base
+anvil --fork-url mainnet --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+`mainnet` is an alias resolved from `packages/foundry/foundry.toml`:
+
+```toml
+mainnet = "https://eth-mainnet.alchemyapi.io/v2/${ALCHEMY_API_KEY}"
+base    = "https://mainnet.base.org"
+```
+
+So the node comes up as a fork of Ethereum mainnet. That is why the reads come
+back empty: Base USDC lives at `0x8335…2913` on Base, and on Ethereum mainnet
+that address is an ordinary EOA-shaped address with no code. `eth_call` into an
+address with no code does not revert — it returns empty data, which viem/ethers
+surface as a decode failure or a zero/empty value. The token looks "not
+deployed" because on the chain we actually forked, it isn't. Mainnet USDC is at
+`0xA0b86991c6218b36c1d19D4a2e9Eb0cE3606eB48` instead.
+
+Everything else looks healthy because it is: the node is a real anvil, it
+answers JSON-RPC, and transactions mine normally. Only the Base-specific state
+is missing.
+
+**The chain-id check cannot catch this.** The Makefile hardcodes
+`--chain-id 31337`, so *every* fork this repo starts — Base, mainnet, anything —
+answers `31337`. Getting "a number back" from `cast chain-id` only proves a node
+is listening; it tells you nothing about what was forked. That is exactly how the
+second person concluded the fork was fine and blamed our contract.
+
+Also worth knowing: `yarn fork --network=base` (with `=`) fails the same way —
+it is still a single token, so it binds to `$0` and `$1` stays empty.
+
+## The command that actually forks Base
+
+Put a token *before* the value so the chain name lands in `$1`:
+
+```bash
+yarn fork --network base   # or: yarn fork -n base   or: yarn fork -- base
+```
+
+Verified here — anvil's startup banner on this checkout:
+
+```
+Endpoint:       https://mainnet.base.org
+Block number:   51663639
+Chain ID:       31337
+Listening on 127.0.0.1:8547
+```
+
+(That run used an explicit `--port` to avoid a busy 8545; `yarn fork --network base`
+uses the default 8545.)
+
+Note the Chain ID is still `31337`. That is expected and is the point of the
+next section.
+
+## A check that actually distinguishes a fork of Base
+
+Do not check the chain id. Check for **state only Base has** — code at a
+Base-specific address:
+
+```bash
+# Base USDC must have code, and must answer symbol()
+cast code 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545 | head -c 20
+cast call 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 'symbol()(string)' --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+```
+
+On a correct Base fork (measured here):
+
+```
+base USDC symbol:      "USDC"
+base USDC code length: 3707 chars   # real bytecode
+mainnet USDC code:     0x           # absent, as it should be on Base
+```
+
+On the mainnet fork `yarn fork base` gives you, both of those flip: Base USDC
+returns `0x` and the `symbol()` call comes back empty.
+
+A second, Base-specific confirmation is the OP-Stack `L1Block` predeploy, which
+exists on Base (and no L1):
+
+```bash
+cast call 0x4200000000000000000000000000000000000015 'number()(uint256)' --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+# -> 26036037   (the L1 block Base is anchored to; reverts/empty on a mainnet fork)
+```
+
+Either check is one line and would have caught this on day one. The rule to
+carry forward: **a fork's chain id is whatever anvil was told to report — verify
+a fork by the state it contains, never by its chain id.**
+
+## Frontend note while doing fork work
+
+Since the fork answers `31337`, `packages/nextjs/scaffold.config.ts`
+`targetNetworks` should stay on `chains.foundry` during fork work — pointing it
+at `chains.base` will not match the local node.

